## 9. A signed prescription is changed only by its own doctor (owner decision 2026-11-16, amends rule 228 §12.2)

1. `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority::signedContentLocked()`: once `doctor_signature_canvas_path` is set, only the
   account `maySign()` accepts for the prescription's doctor (its own `doctor_id`, else the visit's — the doctor the
   edit path derives) may change the prescription. Identity, not role: Super Admin, Owner and Supervisor RME that are
   not that doctor are refused; `Gate::before` lets Super Admin past the policy, so the service decides too.
2. Three layers. `RmePrescriptionPolicy::update` checks the scope (`view()`) FIRST, then the lock, so an out-of-scope
   account is refused for scope and learns nothing about the signature. `RmePrescriptionService::update()` re-reads the
   row after taking the visit lock (every prescription writer takes it first) and refuses with an
   `AuthorizationException` before anything is decoded or written. The page offers no edit to anyone else and says why.
3. A refusal for this reason writes one `RME_PRESCRIPTION_SIGNED_EDIT_REJECTED` row (ids, actor, reason code; no
   value, no patient data): from the controller when the policy's denial message is the lock, from the service after
   the rolled-back transaction otherwise. Other refusals write nothing (rule 228 §9: one row per write, unchanged).
4. The reverse race: the edit form carries `content_version` (`RmePrescriptionService::contentVersion()`, a fingerprint
   of the R/ canvas key and the audited fields). A signature submitted with a version older than the row is refused
   (`MESSAGE_CONTENT_CHANGED_BEFORE_SIGNING`), so a doctor never signs content changed after their page loaded. A
   caller that sends no version is not compared.
5. Unsigned content stays open to the other clinical roles exactly as before (OD-5-1 kept). Tests that exercise what
   ANOTHER clinician may author start from `RmePrescriptionFactory::unsigned()`.

## 10. A new RM sheet only on the authorized encounter (OD-5-6, owner decision 2026-11-16)

Once the patient has an RM book, `MedicalRecordService::createDraft()` refuses a new sheet on any visit other than the
encounter `assertRmeAuthoringAllowedForPatient()` returns (`MESSAGE_SHEET_ONLY_ON_ENCOUNTER`). The patient's first
sheet still starts on the canonical visit the controller derives (Sprint 64.0.2), and the canonical-book path
(`getOrCreateCanonicalMedicalRecord`, `$adoptExisting`) is not a new sheet. The sheet navigation offers "Tambah
Lembar RM" only when the source visit is the patient's current examination (`ActiveEncounterResolver::currentFor`),
still disabled until consent.

## 14. Consent integrity (OD-5-8, OD-5-9, R8A-2, rule 219 §12a) — owner decisions 2026-11-16

1. **One valid consent per visit.** `RmeVisitConsentService::sign()` refuses (`MESSAGE_DUPLICATE`) when the LOCKED visit
   already has a valid consent. Signing again is possible only after a void.
2. **Supervised void** (`rme.consents.void`, POST, `DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN`): dedicated permission
   `void_rme_consents` (Supervisor RME; Super Admin through `Gate::before`), never the signing permission; a written
   reason of 10–1000 characters; only while the visit is `in_progress`; maker ≠ checker — the account that recorded the
   consent (`signed_by`) never voids it, decided in the service under the visit lock so it binds Super Admin too. The
   row is kept (`voided_at`, `voided_by`, `void_reason`); one `RME_CONSENT_VOIDED` audit row with ids and the reason's
   LENGTH only. After a permission change, production runs `PermissionSeeder`, then `RoleSeeder`, then
   `permission:cache-reset` (CG-15).
3. **Rule 219 §12a closed.** Inside a write transaction, `assertRmeAuthoringAllowedForPatient()` locks the encounter's
   visit row FIRST (`lockForStatusDecision`, rule 223 §6) and re-decides status and consent on it. Every consent-gated
   RME write calls it at the top of its transaction: sheet create/update/finalize (`MedicalRecordService`), handwriting
   pages (also the edit-in-place path), structured diagnoses (`MedicalRecordDiagnosisService`), the diagnosis override
   (`DiagnosisRolloutService::grantOverride`). A void or a finish committed meanwhile is seen; a later one waits.
   Odontogram charting does not depend on consent (AIR-RMC-14) and is unaffected.
4. **Authorization before validation (R8A-2).** `StoreRmeVisitConsentRequest::authorize()` and
   `VoidRmeVisitConsentRequest::authorize()` return the policy's `Gate::inspect()` response, so a refused account gets
   its 403/404 whatever its payload.
5. **Storage (OD-5-9, rule 121).** New consent signatures are written to and read from `ClinicalEvidenceStorage`
   (`RmeVisitConsentService::disk()` delegates to it; `config('rme_consent.legacy_signature_disk')` names only the
   pre-change disk for the copy command). No reader falls back to the old disk (rule 121 §5). Objects written before
   the change are copied key-for-key, SHA-256 verified, by `rme:consent-signatures-migrate` (dry run, then `--apply`;
   never deletes — purge is a separate, owner-approved phase, rule 121 §6). The copy runs as the runtime user
   `daengtisiams` and refuses root (private disk: 0700/0600 objects owned by another user are unreadable to PHP-FPM);
   an unreadable object is never copied as empty and never overwritten; a failed write is counted, never printed. A late
   failure inside `sign()` removes the objects that call wrote (rule 224 pattern); a failure after the body finished
   keeps them.
6. **Review round (2026-11-17).** `sign()` and `void()` re-assert the policy's branch and patient scope on the locked
   visit (CG-06 third layer, `MESSAGE_OUT_OF_SCOPE`), before any object is written; `void()` takes the same
   `lockForStatusDecision` as signing. The void form also requires a valid consent on an `in_progress` visit, because
   `Gate::before` answers `void` for Super Admin without the policy. Recorded INFO: an out-of-branch consent answers 403,
   like the existing consent `view`, not 404 (CG-05 drift kept for consistency).
7. Tests: `RmeVisitConsentSupervisedVoidTest` (token `RmeVisitConsent`, declared mandatory).

## 11. A cancelled visit is never a Kontrol prior visit (OD-5-10a)

`ClinicVisitRegistrationGuard::followUpViolation()` refuses a cancelled prior visit (read FOR SHARE under `$lock`, so a
cancellation cannot commit between the check and the insert); `ClinicVisitService::patientVisitOptions()` never
offers one; the visit page offers no "Buat Kontrol" from a cancelled visit (`canStartKontrolFromHere`, decided in the
controller). Existing links are not touched. Billing already refused a cancelled parent for the Rp0 Kontrol.
